# UI and design

Use **shadcn/ui** first (`components/ui`). Add components with `npx shadcn@latest add <name>`. Do not rebuild Button, Input, Table, Dialog, or Form if shadcn has one.

- Style with Tailwind classes and the theme tokens in `styles/globals.css`. Do not use inline `style={{}}` or hard-coded colours.
- Build pages from shadcn primitives plus thin domain components (`PropertyStatusBadge`), not a new design system.
- Forms: every input has a `Label`, errors appear next to the field, `autoComplete` is set where relevant, and forms submit with the keyboard.
- Every control is keyboard-reachable with a visible focus ring, in a logical tab order. Dialogs trap focus, close on Escape, and return focus to what opened them.
- Every list or detail view has **loading**, **empty**, and **error** states. Toasts confirm completed actions; field problems show inline.
- UI wording is for UK letting agents: British spelling, `£`, `DD/MM/YYYY` dates.

## Interaction design and visual coherence

- Design around the user's task, not around individual database fields or server actions. Related changes that users understand as one task should normally share one clear entry point, one review surface, and one save action.
- Keep cards and list rows calm and easy to scan. Avoid adding several adjacent Edit, Add, Remove, or settings buttons when a single **Edit** or **Manage** action can open a well-structured dialog or page.
- Prefer progressive disclosure: show the important current state first, then reveal detailed controls inside the edit flow. Do not make every possible action permanently visible.
- A modal may contain grouped sections or tabs when they belong to the same bounded task and can be understood and saved together. Use a full page instead when the task is long, multi-stage, or needs substantial context. Do not hide unrelated actions in one oversized modal merely to reduce the button count.
- Use one obvious primary action per surface. Secondary and destructive actions must be visually subordinate, clearly labelled, and placed near the item they affect.
- Before adding a new control, inspect the surrounding screen and ask: can this fit naturally into an existing edit/manage flow; will the hierarchy remain clear on mobile; and can the user predict what one save will change?
- Visual quality is part of completion. Check alignment, spacing, hierarchy, wording, empty/error/pending states, keyboard behaviour, and light/dark responsive layouts—not only whether the action technically works.
- When consolidating security-sensitive settings, save the complete validated result atomically where partial updates could briefly or permanently grant unintended access.

## Record pages and surfaces (ADR-030)

- A record page (one person, organisation, property, instruction, or tenancy) uses the **profile sidebar** layout: a left profile column (avatar, name, type, reference, primary actions, key contact lines, then remaining details as icon-led label/value pairs) and a wide working area for related records and Notes. At `lg:` use `grid-cols-[18rem_minmax(0,1fr)]`; below it, stack in the order identity, working area, details.
- Busy records (instruction, property, later tenancy) put **top tabs** across the working area, driven by `?tab=` in the URL so the page stays server-rendered and links open the right tab. Content inside a tab still uses flat sections. Light records (contact) keep one working area (ADR-030 amendment, 2026-10-21).
- Separate sections with headings and hairline dividers (`border-t`, `divide-y`), not boxes. Do not put each group of fields, each list row, or each timeline entry in its own card.
- Use `Card` only for self-contained items: metric tiles, a list or table surface, sign-in and error panels, and choice tiles. Forms in a dialog use flat sections, not nested cards.
- Every detail row has an icon, a muted `text-xs` label and a `text-sm font-medium` value (`dt`/`dd` pairs). Use initials in a circular `bg-accent` avatar for people and organisations until real images exist.
- Grid children that contain long text (emails, addresses) need `min-w-0` or `grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)]` so the page never scrolls sideways. Check at 390px.
- Settings and administration screens are not record pages. Keep their grouped details and branch summaries on white card surfaces, and show lists of users, customers, and similar records as a `Table` inside a card (stacked cards below `md`). Do not flatten them onto the grey canvas.
- Page content **fills the available width** beside the navigation. Do not centre it with `mx-auto` or cap the page wrapper with `max-w-*`; centre only standalone panels such as sign-in, error, not-found, and short choice screens.
- Design layouts that scale with the screen instead of stretching one column: add a rail at `2xl:` (the contact record adds a details column), align list data into columns at `md:`, and lay long forms out in two columns at `xl:` on full pages (not in dialogs). Cap line length only for prose and single-column forms.
- Any element a library hides with an inline `style` attribute is shown on server-rendered pages, because the Content Security Policy blocks inline styles. Hide it with a CSS rule (see the Select input rule in `styles/globals.css`); do not loosen the policy.
- Reference mock-ups: `/design-lab/contact` and `/design-lab/dashboards` (development only; dashboard options are proposals, not adopted).

## Brand (ADR-017)

- Colours are defined once as `--brand-*` variables in `styles/globals.css` and mapped to semantic tokens. In components use semantic classes only (`bg-primary`, `text-muted-foreground`, `bg-sidebar`, `bg-highlight`); never hex, `--brand-*`, or arbitrary `[var(...)]` values. Need a new colour role? Add a semantic token in **both** theme blocks and a contrast pair in `styles/theme-contrast.test.ts`.
- Direction: modern, premium SaaS in the owner-selected **Deep Navy** direction. Light mode uses an off-white canvas, white floating surfaces and restrained blue-grey fills; dark mode uses layered deep-navy surfaces.
- Deep navy is the primary brand colour for actions, links, selected states, focus and the wordmark mark. Use the rounded, softly elevated component treatment selected from the Aubergine Design Lab direction, with the clean conventional table treatment selected from Cobalt. Status colours remain functional, not branding.
- No gradients, bright novelty colours, or traditional estate-agency styling.
- Text needs 4.5:1 contrast (3:1 for large text); control borders and focus outlines need 3:1. Use `border-input` on form controls, `border-border` for dividers.
- Font is Inter (`font-sans`); do not add other typefaces.

## Themes (ADR-018)

- Light is the default. Users choose **Light**, **Dark**, or **System** (follows the device) with `ThemeToggle` (`components/theme/`). The choice is saved in the `zayto-theme` cookie; once accounts exist it will also be saved to the user's profile.
- `:root` holds light tokens, `.dark` holds dark tokens. Every semantic token must exist in both; `npm run test` fails if a pair drops below contrast minimums.
- Status colours: `success`, `warning`, `destructive` (danger), `info`. Solid: `bg-success text-success-foreground`. Subtle: `bg-success-soft text-success`. Never signal status by colour alone; add an icon or text.
- Build every new component for both themes from the start. Prefer tokens that already switch over `dark:` overrides; use `dark:` only when a token can't express it.
- A component is not done until it has been checked in light and dark (browser or screenshot).
- Do not read the theme on the server (`cookies()` in a layout makes every page dynamic). The `<head>` script in `app/layout.tsx` applies it before first paint.

## Responsive layout

Desktop is the main working environment; mobile and tablet must be properly supported, not just shrunk. Design small-screen first, then add `md:` / `lg:` layers.

| Band | Tailwind | Navigation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mobile | default (< 768px) | Top bar with menu button; navigation opens in a slide-out panel (shadcn `Sheet`) |
| Tablet | `md:` (768–1023px) | Same slide-out panel; content may use two columns |
| Desktop | `lg:` (≥ 1024px) | Fixed deep-navy sidebar, collapsible to an icon rail |
| Wide | `xl:` / `2xl:` | Wider content and extra columns; cap reading width with `max-w-*` |

Use Tailwind's default breakpoints only; do not add custom ones. Use container queries (`@container`) when a component's layout depends on its own width rather than the screen's.

- **Tables:** below `md`, show each row as a stacked card with the key fields and a link to the detail page. From `md`, show the table; wide tables scroll horizontally inside their card with the first column pinned. Never let the page itself scroll sideways.
- **Forms:** one column on mobile; two columns from `lg` only where fields belong together (e.g. first and last name). On mobile, keep the main action reachable (sticky footer bar) for long forms.
- **Modals:** centred `Dialog` from `md`; full-height `Sheet` from the bottom on mobile. Long content scrolls inside the modal.
- **Dashboards:** card grids `grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 xl:grid-cols-4`. Charts resize with their container and have a text or table alternative.
- **Filters and toolbars:** collapse into a "Filters" button that opens a sheet on mobile.

## Touch and spacing

- On touch screens (`pointer: coarse`), buttons, inputs, and toggles are at least 44px tall and wide (global rule in `styles/globals.css`). Do not override it. Leave at least 8px between adjacent tap targets.
- Inputs use 16px text on mobile (`text-base md:text-sm`, already in shadcn's `Input`) so iOS does not zoom.
- Never rely on hover alone: anything shown on hover must also be reachable by tap and keyboard.
- Spacing uses Tailwind's 4px scale. Page padding `px-4 md:px-6 lg:px-8`; gaps between sections `gap-6`; inside cards `gap-4`.
- Type scale: page title `text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight`, section title `text-lg font-semibold`, body `text-sm`, captions and meta `text-xs text-muted-foreground`. One page title (`h1`) per page.

- When a design choice is not covered here (layout, wording, which fields to show), ask the owner.

shadcn is configured as Nova / Base UI with `rsc: true`. Global styles load from `app/layout.tsx`.
